speechtext:
Mr. Speaker, today, I welcome this opportunity to table two more petitions asking this Government to listen to the Canadian people, and especially to the many representations I and my party colleagues have made to the Members of the Party in power, the Progressive Conservative Party, all on the subject of proposed tax on food.
Conservatives in the Quebec Caucus have already said they do not want the Government to tax food, and we agree.
Mr. Speaker, the petition I am tabling on behalf of the constituents who visited my riding and especially the By Ward Market this summer, reads as follows:
Whereas since September, 1984, the federal Government has imposed on Canadian taxpayers 25 new taxes or tax increases, in particular on medical and dental supplies, snack foods, candy and soft drinks;
Whereas the federal Government in its tax reform proposal has studied the possibility of imposing on Canadians a general tax on goods and services, including food;
And whereas food constitutes a vital element of day-to-day life, and imposition of this tax would have the effect of seriously penalizing certain
categories of taxpayers, such as the aged, families, the unemployed, low income taxpayers, youth and students;
Wherefore your petitioners humbly pray and call upon Parliament to reject all measures that would have the effect of imposing a sales tax on food products.
[DOT] <1110)

